# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every Scala change. `scripts/check` already enforces formatting,
warnings, unused code, strict equality, Scala 3 syntax and the WartRemover bans, so they are not
repeated here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Plain Scala

Use case classes, enums, pattern matching, `Option`, `Either` and immutable collections, plus the
approved extras below. Type-level programming, implicit conversions, effect libraries
(cats-effect, ZIO) and new type classes need the owner's approval first. Once approved, they go
into the list. Macros are rejected by `scripts/check`.

Approved extras:

- None.

Instances of library type classes are fine, for example `derives CanEqual`.

## Comments

A reader can follow a file from top to bottom by its comments alone.

- Every file starts with a `/** */` comment on its main definition that says what it is for.
- Every public definition has a `/** */` comment that says what it is for and why.
- Explain a Scala construct briefly the first time a file uses it, if the reader may not know it.
  For example `given`, `using`, an extension method or `for` over `Either`. One short sentence,
  sometimes with the equivalent in a language the reader knows.
- No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in words.

## Types and errors

- Every public method states its result type.
- Model choices with an `enum` or a sealed trait, not with strings or booleans.
- Expected failures are values: return `Either` with the module's error type. This code never
  throws (`scripts/check` bans `throw`, `require`, `assert` and `sys.error`). Exceptions come only
  from libraries, for the unexpected, such as a full disk.
- Never drop an error: no `val _ =` or `(x: Unit)` on an `Either` or a `Try`, no
  `Try(...).foreach` or `Try(...).getOrElse(default)`, no `catch case _` or `case _: Throwable`.
  Catch the exception types you expect, and turn them into the module's error type.
- Use the total form of a lookup: `map.get(key)`, `s.toIntOption`, `xs.lift(i)`, also on an
  `Array`, never `it.next()` or `s.charAt(i)` without a check. A `match` on a type that is not
  sealed, such as `Int` or `String`, ends with `case _`.
- No mutable state through Java types either: no `AtomicInteger`, `java.util` collections or
  `array(i) = x`. WartRemover only knows the Scala collections.
- Only `Main.scala` ends the program (`sys.exit`). Library code returns an error instead.
- Take a tuple apart with `match`, not `val (a, b) = x` on a type that is not a tuple: that form
  needs `@unchecked`, which `scripts/check` rejects.

## Functions and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named helper instead of
  nesting.
- Names say what a value is: `environment`, `argument`. Single letters only in tiny lambdas.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new `dep` or `test.dep` line in `project.scala` needs the owner's approval. Give it an exact
version and a comment that says what it is for.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- Every test checks something: an `assert`, `assertEquals` or a property that can fail. A
  property such as `s == s` holds for every input and tests nothing.
- Tests register unconditionally: no `test(...)` inside an `if` or in a loop over a list that may
  be empty.
- A property's generator covers real inputs: no `Gen.resize(0, ...)` or a generator of one value.
- An asynchronous test returns its `Future`, so munit waits for it. A dropped `Future` checks
  nothing.
- Test names describe behaviour.
- Property tests keep the fixed seed. A failing property prints its seed; replay it there.
